  "locateAfterReplace": {
    "note": "A window replaced as a whole (`windows`) and a locate that lands in it, on the component. Rows are messages `m<from>` to `m<to>` in timeline order, all `rowHeight` high, in a viewport `viewportHeight` high (logical pixels); both ends of every window have more to read and the host takes every intent. One run: (1) the list shows `before` with the reader at its bottom; (2) when `readerAskedOlder`, the reader scrolls to the top of `before` and the list asks for an older page, which the host keeps until step 5; (3) the host hands the list `after` (with `windowId` when the case names one); (4) the host asks the list to show `locate`, as the locate trip does once `openAround` has answered, and the list scrolls there; (5) when `pageLands`, the host prepends `pageRows` older rows; (6) when `readerMovesTo` is `top` or `bottom`, the reader scrolls to that end of the window. The rules: a replaced window opens at its newest row; after a replacement the list asks for no page by itself until the reader moves it, and neither the scroll the swap itself causes (a browser may leave the offset at 0 or at the new end), nor the list placing the window, nor a locate landing near an end is the reader moving; a prepend anchor kept for the old window is dropped. A locate takes the reading position: a restore anchored before it is cancelled, a page that lands before the reader moves keeps the located row where the locate put it, and nothing the list does by itself moves the reader off it. `expected.requests` lists every page the list asked for by itself during the run, in order; `expected.locatedInView` is whether the located row is inside the viewport at the end; `expected.marked` is whether it wore the locate mark when the locate landed. A web list runs every case twice, with the offset the swap leaves at 0 and at the end of the new window; a native list runs it once.",
    "rowHeight": 100,
    "viewportHeight": 400,
    "pageRows": 40,
